{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo, with most also having phenotype strings consistent with the disease. However, the set is dominated by framework/portability-style UK Biobank-derived scores (snpnet ‘813 traits’ and ‘Portability of 245 polygenic scores’) plus a small Taiwanese EAS study family with unknown covariates, making it hard to identify a clearly best, disease-focused, externally robust model from the visible evidence.

PGS001267 is preferred because it has (i) a generic AS endpoint (phenotyping_reported: Ankylosing spondylitis) rather than a time-to-event variant, (ii) explicit PRS-comparable performance reported including PGS-only AUROC (0.7265) and PGS-only R² (0.0988) and a full-model AUROC with CI, and (iii) substantially larger European validation sample size (n=67,425) than the EAS-only Taiwanese models (n=1,298). Its PRS-only R² is also plausibly below the provided liability-scale heritability ceiling (h2_liability=0.3993), serving as a sanity check.

Main limitation: PGS001267 (like PGS001268) comes from a pan-trait UKB snpnet framework with an ultra-sparse 10-variant score, which domain guidance flags as potentially less generalizable; meanwhile, an alternative UKB portability score (PGS002089) is much more polygenic and has a higher partial-r (0.0919 vs 0.0797 for PGS001876) but lacks AUROC/R², and the Taiwanese PRS-CS score reports higher full-model AUROC but with unknown covariates and small validation. These unresolved comparability issues keep confidence below High."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept "nodular goiter" via either closely aligned phenotypes ("Nontoxic multinodular goiter": PGS001814, PGS002022) or explicit "Benign nodular goiter" endpoints (PGS005261/PGS005262/PGS005263). The two "thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter" models (PGS005273/PGS005274) are not endpoint-faithful for generic nodular goiter risk because they are cancer-vs-benign discrimination tasks rather than nodular goiter case-control.

Within the endpoint-faithful set, the benign nodular goiter cluster from the thyroid-disease-focused multi-cohort medRxiv study provides a coherent, disease-focused evidence package with large validation (n=94,651) and reported discrimination/effect sizes. Among these, PGS005262 has the highest reported full-model AUROC (0.5933) and the strongest per-SD effect size signal shown (OR 1.408), making it the best-supported pick within that coherent cluster. The UKB portability models (PGS001814/PGS002022) are also phenotype-aligned but come from a portability framework and only report modest partial correlations (0.0277 and 0.024) without AUROC/R2, offering weaker comparative performance evidence.

Limitation: for the benign nodular goiter models the covariates are listed as "Unknown" and the reported AUROC is a full-model metric (not explicitly PGS-only), reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence selection; additionally, the multinodular (nontoxic) vs benign nodular endpoint differences remain a mild phenotype-alignment ambiguity."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are concept-aligned to obesity (trait_efo includes obesity) but differ in endpoint fidelity and strength/comparability of performance evidence. Several are framework-style scores (UKB snpnet across 813 traits; UKB portability across 245 scores), and several newer disease-code/phecode models report only effect sizes without AUROC/R², limiting comparability.

PGS002033 is preferred because it has direct-match labeling (“Overweight, obesity and other hyperalimentation”), large UKB training (n=391,124), and the strongest explicit PRS-comparable performance among the candidates with comparable reported metrics: partial correlation (partial-r)=0.0789 (CI 0.0651–0.0927) in European validation, exceeding the sibling portability model PGS001825 (partial-r=0.0547). Other candidates either (a) provide only full-model metrics or isolated effect sizes without clear PRS-only discrimination (PGS003400, PGS004447, PGS004517, PGS005145, PGS005154, PGS005235, PGS003959), or (b) are time-to-event obesity from a pan-trait UKB snpnet framework with modest PRS-only R²/AUROC (PGS001298), which domain guidance suggests down-ranking when more endpoint-faithful or better-supported alternatives exist.

Main limitation: PGS002033 comes from a portability framework paper and reports partial-r rather than AUROC/R², while many competing models lack directly comparable PRS-only metrics; endpoint specificity is also broader than a pure obesity diagnosis. These constraints prevent a high-confidence, high-quality direct-match designation."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are direct matches via trait_efo=obesity, but they vary in endpoint fidelity and evidence quality. Several candidates are framework/portability scores (PGS001298 snpnet across 813 traits; PGS001825/PGS002033 from the UKB “Portability of 245 polygenic scores” study), while others are disease/phenotype-focused but have sparse or non-comparable metrics (e.g., PGS003400/PGS003959 GWS SNP sets; PGS004447/PGS004517/PGS005235/PGS005145/PGS005154 mainly report effect sizes without PRS-comparable AUROC/R²).

PGS002033 is preferred because it keeps good concept alignment (“Overweight, obesity and other hyperalimentation”) and provides the strongest explicitly PRS-comparable performance signal among the candidates with such metrics: partial correlation (partial-r)=0.0789 (95% CI 0.0651–0.0927) in European validation, outperforming the sibling portability model PGS001825 (partial-r=0.0547). Compared with PGS001298, which does report PRS-only metrics, PGS001298 is a pan-trait UKB snpnet model with a time-to-event phenotype (“TTE obesity”) and only modest PRS-only R²=0.01146/AUC=0.57573; domain guidance indicates such framework snpnet models should be down-ranked when other endpoint-faithful alternatives exist.

Limitation: the overall evidence is still limited and heterogeneous—most non-framework candidates lack AUROC/R², some have unclear/unknown covariates, and endpoints differ (TTE obesity, BMI-derived, ICD10 E66, phecode 278.1). Because the selected model comes from a portability framework rather than a clearly disease-focused obesity PRS study, and because comparable metrics are not available across the full set, the best choice cannot be made with high confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are concept-level direct matches to obesity (trait_efo includes obesity; several also use ICD10 E66 or an obesity phecode). However, most have limited or non-comparable performance reporting (many show only an OR/beta without AUROC/R², or only a full-model R² with unknown covariates), lowering the ability to make a clearly best-supported choice.

PGS002033 is preferred because it has good endpoint alignment ("Overweight, obesity and other hyperalimentation"), comes with an explicit PRS-comparable performance metric (partial correlation partial-r=0.0789 with CI) and uses standard demographic/PC covariates in a large UKB-derived training set (n=391,124) with stated European validation context. Among the two otherwise similar portability-framework models (PGS001825 vs PGS002033), PGS002033 has the stronger reported partial-r.

Key limitation: PGS002033 (and PGS001825) are from a UKB portability/framework study rather than a disease-focused obesity PRS development, and several other direct-match candidates lack directly comparable metrics (e.g., only ORs or unclear full-model metrics), so this selection cannot be made with high confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005219","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for obstructive sleep apnea are present, including multiple OSA-labeled scores (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220) plus broader “sleep apnea” ExPRSweb framework scores (PGS003204–PGS003218). Per domain guidance, prefer endpoint/phenotype fidelity and disease-focused evidence over pan-trait framework scores; the ExPRSweb models are framework-origin and show near-chance full-model AUROCs (~0.49–0.53), so they are weakly supported here.

Among OSA-labeled candidates, PGS005219 (Obstructive sleep apnea, AdjustedBMI) has the strongest visible performance signal (full_model_auc=0.79; OR per SD=1.98, 95% CI 1.74–2.24) and comes from a disease-focused OSA PRS paper with multi-cohort development cohorts listed (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) and a moderate validation size (n=21,975) with multi-ancestry evaluation noted. Compared with the MVP genome-wide-significant SNP scores (PGS003857/PGS003858; AUROC=0.77) and the smaller HCHS/SOL score (PGS003479; small n=1,115 and no AUROC), PGS005219 has slightly stronger reported discrimination and effect size support in the provided metadata.

Key limitation: the reported AUROCs appear to be full-model metrics (not explicitly PRS-only), and BMI is included as a covariate in the evaluation, which reduces comparability to PRS-only performance; additionally, PGS005219 is BMI-adjusted (phenotype/definition variant), so endpoint alignment is not perfectly “generic OSA” despite being a direct OSA concept. These issues prevent a high-confidence, high-quality designation."}
